<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Chelenqo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1887">1887</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Chelenqo was fought on 6 January 1887 in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> between the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menelik-ii">Menelik II</a>, then king of Shewa, and the forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emir">Emir</a> Abdullahi of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>. Menelik's victory led to the occupation of Harar and its annexation to his realm, a key step in the southern and eastern expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopian-empire">Ethiopian Empire</a>. His cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a> Makonnen, father of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haile-selassie">Haile Selassie</a>, became governor of Harar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-chelenqo.jpg" alt="Battle of Chelenqo" width="320" height="211" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abyssinian_painting_of_the_battle_of_chelenqo.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>In response to Italian control of parts of Mereb Melash and the port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/massawa">Massawa</a>, Menelik had begun to import firearms and munitions through the French-controlled ports of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/djibouti">Djibouti</a>, and by 1886 the emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> had blocked their transport through his territory. The killing of the Italian explorer Count Pietro Porro and his whole party in April 1886, allegedly on the emir's orders, gave Menelik a pretext to march on a city he had long wanted to control.</p><p>A Shewan general was defeated by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s forces at the Battle of Hirna late in 1886. Menelik then invaded again, offering the emir the kind of autonomy that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abba-jifar-ii">Abba Jifar II</a> enjoyed in the Kingdom of Jimma, and the emir refused. His army was recruited from the city's four quarters: Assum Beri, Asma'din Bari, Badro Bari and Suqutat Bari.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The Harari Chronicle records an exchange of challenges before the fighting, Menelik pointing to his numbers and the emir of the strength of his men. Heavily outnumbered and armed mostly with obsolete <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matchlock">matchlocks</a> and a few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannons</a>, the emir attacked on the Ethiopian Christmas, expecting the Shewans to be unprepared after the feast, but Menelik had kept his men on alert. Makonnen's column destroyed the Harari artillery, and within twenty minutes the Shewans had won.</p><p>The historian Marcus judged that the emir might have done better to let the Shewans attack the walled city, where his few <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krupp">Krupp</a> cannon could have been effective. Menelik pursued him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a>. The emir fled with his wives and children into the desert east of the city, leaving his uncle Ali Abu Bakar to negotiate its surrender, and Harar's independence came to an end.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>According to Jules Borelli, Abyssinian soldiers pillaged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harar">Harar</a> and half its population fled. Makonnen Wolde Mikael, made governor and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ras">Ras</a>, expropriated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harari">Harari</a> land for his soldiers, and highland Christians settled as a new ruling elite. When the Harari revolted, his troops plundered homes and massacred civilians. Richard Pankhurst writes that the Harari were left with token representation, and the central mosque was destroyed and the Medhane Alem Church built in its place.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Chelenqo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Menelik II defeated him at the <b>Battle of Chelenqo</b> in 1887 and annexed Harar to the Ethiopian Empire.</blockquote><a href="/state/harar-emirate">Harar Emirate</a></li></ul></section>
